    "original_title": "Guddiga Miisaaniyadda oo dib-u-eegis ku bilaabay Hindise-sharciyeedka wax-ka-beddelka Sharciga Bankiga Dhexe",
    "slug": "somali-parliament-committee-reviews-central-bank-law-amendment",
    "summary": "The Somali Federal Parliament's Budget and Finance Committee met with the Finance Minister and Central Bank Governor to review proposed amendments to the Central Bank Act.",
    "content": "On September 22, 2026, the Budget, Finance, Planning, International Cooperation, and Government Accountability Committee of Somalia's Federal Parliament held a consultative meeting at Villa Hargeisa in Mogadishu to review the proposed amendment to the Central Bank of Somalia Act. The meeting was chaired by MP Cabdi Ibraahim Warsame (Qawdhan) and included Finance Minister Biixi Iimaan Cige and the Governor of the Central Bank.\n\nThe discussion centered on the legislative amendment to the Central Bank Act, which is part of broader efforts to strengthen Somalia's financial institutions and regulatory framework. The committee's review is a key step in the legislative process before the bill can proceed to a full parliamentary vote.\n\nSomalia has been working to rebuild its financial sector after decades of conflict and instability. The Central Bank plays a critical role in monetary policy, currency issuance, and oversight of commercial banks. The proposed amendments are expected to enhance the bank's independence and governance, aligning with international best practices.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, which sends billions of dollars in remittances annually, a robust and transparent Central Bank is essential. The diaspora relies on formal financial channels for remittances, and a strengthened regulatory environment can reduce risks and costs associated with money transfers. Additionally, diaspora investors looking to contribute to Somalia's reconstruction are more likely to engage when financial institutions are well-regulated.\n\nThe committee's review is the latest in a series of reforms aimed at modernizing Somalia's financial system. In recent years, the government has taken steps to improve financial governance, including the passage of anti-money laundering laws and efforts to license mobile money providers. The Central Bank Act amendment is seen as a cornerstone of these ongoing reforms.\n\nOnce the committee completes its review, the bill will be forwarded to the full Parliament for debate and potential approval. If enacted, the amendments could significantly shape Somalia's economic trajectory and its integration into the global financial system.\n\nRead the original report from Goobjoog here: https://goobjoog.com/2026/09/22/guddiga-miisaaniyadda-oo-dib-u-eegis-ku-bilaabay-hindise-sharciyeedka-wax-ka-beddelka-sharciga-bankiga-dhexe/",
