    "title": "Somali Parliament Committee Urges Expansion of NIRA Registration Centers",
    "original_title": "Golaha Shacabka oo dalbaday in la kordhiyo goobaha laga qaato Aqoonsiga NIRA",
    "slug": "somali-parliament-committee-urges-expansion-of-nira-registration-centers",
    "summary": "A parliamentary committee in Mogadishu met with the Interior Minister and NIRA director, calling for more registration sites to improve access to national ID cards.",
    "content": "A committee of Somalia's federal parliament, responsible for internal affairs, regional administrations, and security, convened on Monday in Mogadishu with Interior Minister Ali Yusuf Ali (Ali Hosh) and the director of the National Identification and Registration Authority (NIRA), Abdiwali Ali Abdulle. The meeting, chaired by MP Nur Ahmed Diriye (Nur Afey), the committee's deputy chair, focused on the operations of NIRA and the progress of national ID registration. The committee heard briefings from both officials and subsequently recommended that the number of registration centers be increased across the country.\n\nNIRA was established to provide a unified national identity system, a key component of Somalia's state-building efforts. The agency has faced logistical and security challenges in rolling out registration nationwide, particularly in areas recently liberated from al-Shabab control. The call to expand registration sites is aimed at addressing overcrowding and long travel distances that many citizens face when seeking IDs.\n\nThe parliamentary committee's recommendation comes amid broader government efforts to enhance service delivery and strengthen federal-regional coordination. The Interior Ministry has been at the forefront of these initiatives, working to improve security and governance. The meeting reflects ongoing legislative oversight of executive agencies and a push to make identity documentation more accessible.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, the expansion of NIRA registration centers is significant. Many diaspora members require Somali national IDs to access property, inherit assets, or participate in elections. A more widespread and efficient registration system could facilitate these processes and strengthen ties between the diaspora and their homeland. The committee's recommendation, if implemented, may also streamline diaspora registration procedures, which have historically been cumbersome.\n\nThe committee did not announce a specific timeline for the expansion, but its recommendation will be forwarded to the relevant authorities for consideration. The move is part of a larger effort to improve public services and ensure that all Somalis, including those abroad, can obtain official identification. Further discussions are expected in parliament regarding the implementation of this proposal.\n\nRead the original report from Goobjoog here: https://goobjoog.com/2026/09/22/golaha-shacabka-oo-dalbaday-in-la-kordhiyo-goobaha-laga-qaato-aqoonsiga-nira/",
