    "summary": "Foreign ministers from G7 countries have called on Yemen's Houthi movement to immediately cease military operations targeting Saudi Arabia and commercial shipping, while also urging Iran to stop arming the group.",
    "content": "Foreign ministers from the Group of Seven (G7) industrialized nations have issued a joint statement calling on the Houthi movement in Yemen to immediately halt all military operations directed at Saudi Arabia. The ministers also condemned recent attacks originating from Yemen that have targeted the kingdom, and demanded an end to strikes on civilian and international commercial vessels in the Red Sea. The appeal comes amid rising tensions in the Middle East, with the G7 expressing deep concern over the escalating crisis.\n\nThe statement further urged Iran to cease its military support for the Houthis, including the supply of weapons and financial assistance. The G7 has long expressed alarm over Iran's role in regional conflicts, particularly its backing of non-state armed groups. While the Houthis have not publicly responded to the latest call, previous similar appeals have gone unheeded.\n\nThe Houthi movement has controlled large parts of Yemen since 2014 and has been engaged in a protracted conflict with a Saudi-led coalition since 2015. In recent years, the group has launched numerous drone and missile attacks on Saudi territory, as well as assaults on shipping lanes in the Red Sea and Gulf of Aden. These actions have disrupted global trade and raised fears of a wider regional war. Diplomatic efforts to broker a lasting ceasefire have repeatedly faltered.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, the stability of the Red Sea corridor is of vital importance. Many Somalis rely on maritime trade routes for the import of essential goods, including food and medicine, and any disruption can lead to price hikes and shortages. Moreover, Somalia has its own security challenges, and a broader regional conflagration could divert international attention and resources away from supporting stability in the Horn of Africa. The diaspora, with its strong ties to both Somalia and the wider region, watches such developments with heightened concern.\n\nThe G7's call reflects growing international pressure to de-escalate tensions, but it remains uncertain whether it will yield concrete results. The Houthis have previously dismissed similar appeals, and Iran has consistently denied providing military support to the group. As the situation evolves, the international community continues to seek a diplomatic resolution to prevent further destabilization of the Middle East.\n\nRead the original report from Goobjoog here: https://goobjoog.com/2026/09/22/g7-oo-xuutiyiinta-ugu-baaqay-inay-si-degdeg-ah-u-joojiyaan-weerarrada-ka-dhanka-ah-sacuudiga/",
